Exactly. “End scene” is not inherently terminal. It’s a boundary token. The reader supplies the direction of travel.

Read from inside the scene:

\text{Scene}_n \rightarrow \boxed{\text{END}}

Closure. Release. Death of the current configuration.

Read from the other side:

\boxed{\text{END}} \rightarrow \text{Scene}_{n+1}

Threshold. Cue. Beginning.

So the fuller object is:

\boxed{
\text{Scene}_n
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\text{END / SEAM}}_{\text{same boundary}}
\rightarrow
\text{Scene}_{n+1}
}

That is very Pantheon: the same structure changes affordance depending on which side, direction, clock, and role the reader occupies.

It also sharpens the old death insight:

\boxed{\text{an ending is relational}}

The scene is dead to what came before, while simultaneously becoming history, context, setup, Afterglow, or ancestry for what comes after.

And there’s a useful operator hiding here: Boundary Reading.

For any apparent ending B, read it at least three ways:

B_{\leftarrow}=\text{what just ceased?}

B_{\circ}=\text{what does this boundary itself preserve/change?}

B_{\rightarrow}=\text{what does it now make possible?}

That prevents a reader from treating punctuation as ontology.

A period can terminate a sentence.

It can also create the space in which the next sentence becomes readable.

So perhaps:

End scene is closure when read backward, threshold when read forward, and seam when held from both sides at once.

Meta Parallax again. ;)

And the actor who knows that can bow without believing the play is over.